m/pyproject.toml\ntests/data/src/pep518-3.0/setup.cfg\ntests/data/src/pep518_twin_forkbombs_second-238/pyproject.toml\ntests/data/src/pep518_missing_requires/pyproject.toml\ntests/data/src/pep518_twin_forkbombs_second-238/setup.cfg\ntests/data/src/pep518_conflicting_requires/pyproject.toml\ntests/data/src/simplewheel-1.0/setup.cfg\ntests/data/src/simplewheel-2.0/setup.cfg\ntests/data/src/requires_simple_extra/setup.cfg\ntests/data/src/singlemodule/setup.cfg\ntests/data/src/withpyproject/pyproject.toml\ntests/data/src/pep518_twin_forkbombs_first-234/pyproject.toml\ntests/data/src/pep518_twin_forkbombs_first-234/setup.cfg\n# APOSD for Codex\n\nAPOSD means applying John Ousterhout's \"A Philosophy of Software Design\" as an operating discipline for AI-assisted coding. The goal is not prettier code. The goal is to prevent incremental complexity from accumulating while agents move quickly.\n\n## Core mandate\n\nComplexity is anything that makes code hard to understand or change. It accumulates one reasonable-looking patch at a time. Fight it on every change.\n\nBe strategic, not tactical. Spend the extra time to keep the design clean instead of only making the immediate task work.\n\n## Module rules\n\n- Prefer deep modules: powerful behavior behind a simple interface.\n- A simple interface matters more than a simple implementation.\n- Pull complexity downward. Callers should not know implementation policy.\n- Hide information. Each module should encapsulate a design decision.\n- Prefer somewhat general-purpose interfaces over narrowly special-purpose ones when the general form stays simple.\n- Adjacent layers should use different abstractions.\n- Avoid pass-through methods and pass-through variables that add no value.\n\n## Error and special-case rules\n\n- Define errors out of existence when possible. Change the API or flow so fewer callers need to handle exceptional cases.\n- Minimize special cases. The normal path should handle as much as possible.\n- Do not spread one policy across many branches, flags, or call sites.\n\n## Comments and names\n\n- Comments should explain what is not obvious from the code: why, contract, invariant, units, ownership, concurrency, edge-case rationale.\n- Do not write comments that narrate what the code already says.\n- Separate interface comments from implementation comments.\n- Names are abstractions. Make them precise, consistent, and unambiguous.\n- If a name is hard to choose, or its comment must be vague/long, treat that as a design smell.\n\n## Red flags\n\nStop and reconsider when any of these appear:\n\n- shallow module\n- information leakage\n- temporal decomposition\n- overexposure\n- pass-through method\n- pass-through variable\n- repetition\n- special-general mixture\n- conjoined methods\n- comment that repeats the code\n- implementation detail leaking into interface comment\n- vague or hard-to-pick name\n- nonobvious code\n\n## Codex workflow\n\nBefore nontrivial implementation:\n\n1. Name the design decision being changed.\n2. Identify the module boundary that should own the complexity.\n3. State the acceptance gates.\n\nDuring implementation:\n\n1. Prefer moving complexity into one deep module over spreading small conditionals across callers.\n2. Keep interfaces smaller than implementations.\n3. Delete pass-through abstractions unless they create a real boundary.\n\nBefore final response:\n\n1. Report the artifact.\n2. Report checks run.\n3. Report any APOSD tradeoff or red flag that remains.\n4. If verification could not run, say the work is not fully shippable.\n\n## Existing Claude-side enforcement\n\nClaude has an APOSD enforcement system at `/home/Yatsuiii/.claude/aposd-system.md`:\n\n- L1 per-edit deterministic lint floor through `~/.claude/hooks/clean_code_floor.py`\n- L2 pre-commit APOSD diff review through `~/.claude/git-hooks/pre-commit`\n- usage logging in `~/.claude/aposd-usage.log`\n\nCodex should respect those gates when working in repositories that use them. Do not bypass them unless Raghav explicitly asks.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
